  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday...\n\nShortwave energy moving through behind a low pressure system to\nthe northeast is currently bringing mountain snow to area \nmountains of south central MT and northern WY. This snow is \nexpected to continue through the morning and move into the \nfoothills as low to mid level winds become northerly, favoring an \nupslope pattern for the Red Lodge and Beartooth foothills. The \nAbsaroka/Beartooths are expected to get an additional 6-12 inches \nwith the associated foothills expected to receive up to 6 inches \nalong the higher foothills. The Bighorn Mountains are forecast to \nget an additional 5-10 inches with the Crazies at around 3-6 \ninches. Use caution if recreating in the high country or traveling\nalong the foothills through the morning. \n\nDryer air moves in late Saturday with moisture moving in again\nas several weak waves of energy move through late Sunday through\nTuesday. This will mainly bring chances for precipitation to the \nmountains across south central MT with isolated showers across the\nplains. An upper level low looks to move into the southwest US, \nleaving this region with a lack of forcing. Clusters generally \nkeeping the region under ridging for the latter half of the week, \nsignaling warm and dry conditions. \n\nPeriods of breezy conditions will return for the Livingston/Nye \nareas Saturday night through Monday as westerly flow aloft favors \na surface pressure gradient across the Absaroka Beartooths. With a\ngradient from KIDA to KLWT of around 12-15 mb and around 15-20 kt\n700 mb winds, winds aren't expected to be strong. At this time,\nthe most likely wind gusts will be 30-50 mph, strongest Sunday \nmorning. There is currently a 40% chance for gusts to 50 mph \nduring this time. \n\nHigh temperatures will remain slightly above to near average in\nthe 30s and 40s, warming into the low 50s by Tuesday. TS",
